# Aberration — Every system has a pattern.

Anomaly detection for physical and digital systems.

Detect when strain, traffic, flow, quality, yield, efficiency, precision or pressure changes. Continuous anomaly detection for sensor telemetry and digital data streams.

- Dashboard: {{ frontend_base_url }}/

## At a glance

| Workspace | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| Sources | Connect MQTT sources and choose which readings to record |
| Monitoring | Design and run visual detection pipelines |
| Reporting | Inspect changes alongside their readings and detection evidence |
| Dojo | Replay recorded data, compare runs and refine a design |

The hero cycles through generated photographs of a steel bridge, a data-centre cable runway, an industrial pipe rack, a powered roller conveyor, a solar canopy, an industrial cooling bank, an overhead robotic gantry and a centre-pivot irrigation span. These illustrate connected infrastructure; they are not photographs of Aberration hardware or customer installations. The product screenshots show the actual visual composer and Reporting interface. The cooling-circuit configuration and telemetry are illustrative examples, not customer data or a performance benchmark.

## From raw signal. To informed decision.

Bring your readings into one workspace. Build monitors around your system, investigate the changes they flag, and test your next design on recorded data.

## Available pipeline components

- **36 detection models:** statistical, machine-learning and forecast-based detection.
- **26 signal transforms:** prepare, scale and project the readings that matter.
- **16 calibration methods:** give anomaly scores a meaningful decision boundary.

These are component counts from the application's catalogue. Detection models include batch, adaptive and forecast-residual implementations; pretrained forecasting requires its model checkpoint to be available.

## Build the logic. See every step.

Connect your readings, prepare the inputs and run detectors in parallel. Calibrate their scores, choose a detection rule and keep the evidence. Every step is visible and configurable.

The actual visual composer shows an example cooling circuit: pressure, flow and temperature inputs pass through standard scaling, parallel Isolation Forest and Local Outlier Factor detectors, a conformal detection rule and evidence output.

## See the change. Keep the context.

A flag is the start of an investigation. Follow it back to the readings, inspect the detection evidence and see how the system behaved before and after.

- **Collect the right signals.** Connect MQTT sources, organise their readings and choose what to record. Monitor one signal or a set of related inputs.
- **See why it was flagged.** Review readings and detection evidence on a shared timeline. Zoom into a transient, a gradual drift or a change in operating behaviour.
- **Keep improving the monitor.** Check its operational status, copy recorded readings into the Dojo and test an adjusted design against the same history.

The Reporting screenshot uses illustrative cooling-circuit data with operating variation, a pressure transient and a developing restriction.

## Try it on the past. Before it watches the present.

Replay recorded readings or a CSV through a detection pipeline in the Dojo. See what it would have flagged, compare your options, and take a tested design into monitoring.

1. **Replay — give a design some history.** Choose recorded data or upload a dataset. Replay it in time order through the same detection logic used in live monitoring.
2. **Compare — find out what it catches.** Label known events and compare runs. Look at detected changes, detection delays, and false alarms together.
3. **Refine — take the better design forward.** Adjust the pipeline, run it again, and reuse the design in monitoring when you are ready.

Recorded data → test and compare → live monitoring. One workflow. Room to improve.
